Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is an important step in understanding hyperspectral datasets. It involves examining spectral measurements, identifying patterns, and understanding the distribution of data before applying Machine Learning techniques.

Understanding Spectral Data Distribution

Analyzing the distribution of spectral values helps understand how reflectance measurements vary across different wavelength bands. This provides useful insights into the characteristics of hyperspectral data.

Spectral Data Visualization

Data visualization helps represent spectral information through graphical techniques. Line plots, histograms, and distribution plots can be used to explore spectral variations and identify patterns in crop health data.

Identifying Patterns and Relationships

EDA helps explore relationships between spectral features and crop health conditions. It can also help identify unusual observations, variations, and potential patterns associated with healthy and diseased crops.

Importance of EDA in TerraSpectra

- Understands hyperspectral dataset characteristics.
- Identifies patterns and variations in spectral measurements.
- Helps detect missing values and unusual observations.
- Supports feature analysis and selection.
- Provides insights for Machine Learning model development.

